{
  "name": "creds-for-devs",
  "displayName": "CredsForDevs",
  "description": "Zero-trust credential manager for developers: SSH hosts and keys, VPN configs, databases, scripts and the CLI commands nobody remembers — in VS Code. Secrets live in the OS keychain; sync and team sharing are end-to-end encrypted, so the self-hosted vault server never sees plaintext. An AI coding agent can use an SSH credential without ever receiving it.",
  "version": "0.64.0",
  "publisher": "remsoftdev",
  "license": "MIT",
  "icon": "media/icon.png",
  "engines": {
    "vscode": "^1.85.0"
  },
  "categories": [
    "Other"
  ],
  "keywords": [
    "ssh",
    "credentials",
    "password manager",
    "secrets",
    "vault",
    "zero trust",
    "end-to-end encryption",
    "yubikey",
    "self-hosted",
    "vpn",
    "wireguard",
    "openvpn",
    "database",
    "ssh keys",
    "claude code",
    "ai agent"
  ],
  "repository": {
    "type": "git",
    "url": "https://github.com/oleksandrdubyna88/dew_flow_creds_for_devs.git",
    "directory": "src_vs_code"
  },
  "bugs": {
    "url": "https://github.com/oleksandrdubyna88/dew_flow_creds_for_devs/issues"
  },
  "homepage": "https://github.com/oleksandrdubyna88/dew_flow_creds_for_devs#readme",
  "galleryBanner": {
    "color": "#1F4E96",
    "theme": "dark"
  },
  "main": "./dist/extension.js",
  "extensionKind": [
    "ui"
  ],
  "scripts": {
    "compile": "tsc -p ./",
    "watch": "tsc -w -p ./",
    "typecheck": "tsc -p ./ --noEmit",
    "lint": "eslint src",
    "test": "npm run compile && node --test \"out/test/*.test.js\"",
    "itest:server": "npm run compile && node scripts/server-transport-itest.cjs",
    "itest:agent": "npm run compile && node scripts/agent-broker-itest.cjs",
    "itest:ssh-agent": "npm run compile && node scripts/ssh-agent-itest.cjs",
    "itest:masked-run": "npm run compile && node scripts/masked-run-itest.cjs",
    "icon": "node scripts/generate-icon.mjs",
    "bundle": "npm run compile && esbuild out/extension.js out/agentCli.js --bundle --platform=node --target=node18 --format=cjs --external:vscode --outdir=dist --sourcemap",
    "vscode:prepublish": "npm run bundle",
    "package": "vsce package",
    "publish": "vsce publish",
    "itest:git": "npm run compile && node scripts/git-transport-itest.cjs",
    "contract": "npm run compile && node scripts/emit-contract.mjs",
    "itest:cli": "npm run compile && node scripts/creds-cli-itest.cjs"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@types/node": "^20.11.0",
    "@types/vscode": "^1.85.0",
    "@vscode/vsce": "^3.0.0",
    "esbuild": "^0.28.2",
    "eslint": "^10.9.1",
    "typescript": "^5.4.0",
    "typescript-eslint": "^8.68.0"
  },
  "contributes": {
    "configuration": {
      "title": "CredsForDevs",
      "properties": {
        "credSshManager.nasBackupPath": {
          "type": "string",
          "default": "",
          "markdownDescription": "Default vault **location**: either a folder for the encrypted `vault_<email>.enc` files (e.g. `/mnt/z/Backups`, `Z:\\Backups`, `\\\\NAS\\Vault`) **or** a Cred Vault Server URL (`https://vault.company.com`) — a URL switches that account to authenticated server sync."
        },
        "credSshManager.accountNasPaths": {
          "type": "object",
          "default": {},
          "additionalProperties": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "markdownDescription": "Per-account vault location: maps an account **email** to a folder **or** a Cred Vault Server URL, e.g. `{ \"work@corp.com\": \"https://vault.corp.com\", \"me@gmail.com\": \"/mnt/home-nas/vault\" }`. Accounts without a mapping use `#credSshManager.nasBackupPath#`. Easiest way to set: right-click the account → *Set Sync Location…*"
        },
        "credSshManager.autoSync": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "default": false,
          "markdownDescription": "Automatically sync all profiles with the NAS (`#credSshManager.nasBackupPath#`): after every change, on startup, and on an interval. Changes from several machines are MERGED per node (newest edit wins; deletions propagate). Requires the same sync PIN on every machine (`CredsForDevs: Set Sync PIN`)."
        },
        "credSshManager.autoSyncIntervalMinutes": {
          "type": "number",
          "default": 5,
          "minimum": 1,
          "markdownDescription": "How often auto-sync pulls the NAS files, in minutes."
        },
        "credSshManager.dbExtensions": {
          "type": "object",
          "default": {},
          "additionalProperties": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "markdownDescription": "Per-DB-type extension override for the Connect action, e.g. `{ \"mysql\": \"cweijan.vscode-mysql-client2\" }`. When empty, the first installed candidate is used (Database Client, the Microsoft SQL/PostgreSQL extensions, MongoDB for VS Code, …)."
        },
        "credSshManager.googleClientId": {
          "type": "string",
          "default": "",
          "markdownDescription": "OAuth 2.0 client id of your **Desktop app** credential (Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials). Required for \"Sign in with Google\". The client secret is prompted on first sign-in and kept in SecretStorage."
        },
        "credSshManager.backupLocation": {
          "type": "string",
          "default": "",
          "markdownDescription": "Where **dated snapshots** of the vault are written, e.g. `/mnt/nas/vault-history`, `Z:\\Backups`, or a Google Drive / OneDrive sync folder. Leave empty to disable.\n\nThis is deliberately **separate from `#credSshManager.nasBackupPath#`**. That one is the *live* vault and it MERGES — delete a credential and the deletion travels to every machine. Snapshots are the safety net you restore from when the deletion was the mistake, so pointing both at the same disk defeats the point.\n\nSnapshots are the same encrypted bytes the sync location holds; no PIN is needed to take one, and one is restored with *Import / Restore*."
        },
        "credSshManager.accountBackupPaths": {
          "type": "object",
          "default": {},
          "additionalProperties": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "markdownDescription": "Per-account snapshot folder: maps an account **email** to a folder. Accounts without a mapping use `#credSshManager.backupLocation#`. Easiest way to set: right-click the account → *Set Backup Location…*"
        },
        "credSshManager.backupIntervalHours": {
          "type": "number",
          "default": 24,
          "minimum": 0,
          "markdownDescription": "How often a snapshot is taken, in hours. `24` daily, `168` weekly, `1` hourly. **`0` disables snapshots.**\n\nA snapshot whose bytes are identical to the previous one is not written, so a quiet vault does not fill a metered folder with copies of itself."
        },
        "credSshManager.backupRetainDays": {
          "type": "number",
          "default": 30,
          "minimum": 0,
          "markdownDescription": "Delete snapshots older than this. `0` keeps them forever. The **newest snapshot is never deleted**, whatever its age — a laptop closed for a year must not come back to an empty backup folder."
        },
        "credSshManager.autoLockMinutes": {
          "type": "number",
          "default": 60,
          "minimum": 0,
          "markdownDescription": "Lock the vaults after this many minutes **without you using them**. **`0` disables auto-lock.**\n\n\"Using them\" means an action of yours that touches a stored secret — opening or copying a credential, connecting, installing a key, editing an entry, or unlocking. It is **not** mouse movement, and it is **not** background sync: a sync cycle running on a timer is not you being present, so it does not postpone the lock. (It used to, which made this setting do nothing at all once `#credSshManager.autoSync#` was switched on.)\n\nLocking forgets the cached master key *and* refuses the saved Sync PIN until you unlock deliberately. Background sync pauses and says so; your credentials keep working locally, because they live in the OS keychain and are not protected by the vault key."
        },
        "credSshManager.readCliHelp": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "default": true,
          "markdownDescription": "When you paste a whole command into a terminal entry, read what each flag means by running `<tool> --help` and filling the empty notes.\n\nIt runs the tool **you just typed**, with no shell and no arguments of ours, and only when every word of the command is a plain tool name — anything containing a shell metacharacter is never run. Empty notes only: what you wrote is never overwritten.\n\nTurn it off if you would rather nothing was executed while you edit an entry; the command is still split into rows, and you write the notes yourself."
        },
        "credSshManager.accountBackupIntervals": {
          "type": "object",
          "default": {},
          "additionalProperties": {
            "type": "number",
            "minimum": 0
          },
          "markdownDescription": "Per-account snapshot schedule, in **hours**: maps an account **email** to an interval, e.g. `{ \"work@corp.com\": 24, \"me@gmail.com\": 168 }`. `0` switches snapshots off for that account. Accounts without a mapping use `#credSshManager.backupIntervalHours#`.\n\nEasiest way to set: right-click the account -> *Set Backup Schedule…* — it offers hourly, every 6 hours, daily, weekly, off, or a custom number."
        },
        "credSshManager.microsoftApiScope": {
          "type": "string",
          "default": "",
          "markdownDescription": "The API scope of **your organisation's own Entra app registration**, e.g. `api://<client-id>/vault.access`. Required for Microsoft sign-in against a Cred Vault Server.\n\nWhy: without this the extension sends a Microsoft **Graph** access token (`user.read`), and Graph tokens are deliberately unverifiable by third parties — every server rejects them with 401. A token minted for your own app registration is an ordinary validatable JWT.\n\nOne-time setup (Entra admin): **App registrations → New** (single tenant) → **Expose an API** → set Application ID URI `api://<client-id>` → **Add a scope** `vault.access` → **Add a client application** `aebc6443-996d-45c2-90f0-388ff96faa56` (Visual Studio Code) authorised for that scope. Then set the server's `MS_AUDIENCES=<client-id>` and put `api://<client-id>/vault.access` here."
        },
        "credSshManager.breachCheck": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "default": false,
          "markdownDescription": "Let the **Health Report** check your stored passwords against the [Have I Been Pwned](https://haveibeenpwned.com/Passwords) corpus.\n\n**Off by default, because this is the only feature that uses the network for anything other than your own sync location.** With it on, the report still asks before each run, and states what is sent.\n\nWhat is sent, precisely: the **first five characters of each password's SHA-1** — one bucket out of 2²⁰, shared by hundreds of thousands of passwords. The service returns the whole bucket and the match is made on this machine, so it cannot know which password was asked about. The password itself, and the remaining 35 characters of its hash, never leave."
        },
        "credSshManager.secretClipboardTtlSeconds": {
          "type": "number",
          "default": 45,
          "minimum": 5,
          "markdownDescription": "How long a copied secret stays on the clipboard before CredsForDevs clears it — but only if the clipboard still holds exactly what was copied, so a later copy of your own is never destroyed.\n\n**What this cannot control:** the clipboard belongs to the operating system. Windows Clipboard History (Win+V) and cross-device sync can capture a value the moment it is copied, and clearing the clipboard afterwards does not remove it from there. Turn those off if you copy secrets on a machine you do not control."
        },
        "credSshManager.agentGrantIdleMinutes": {
          "type": "number",
          "default": 60,
          "minimum": 0,
          "markdownDescription": "How long a **Share with Claude Code** token stays valid without being used, in minutes. A token an agent is actively using stays live; one it forgot about — pasted into a transcript that outlives the task — goes dead on its own. `0` means no idle limit; every token still dies when this window closes."
        },
        "credSshManager.agentGrantMaxCalls": {
          "type": "number",
          "default": 0,
          "minimum": 0,
          "markdownDescription": "The most calls one **Share with Claude Code** token may make before it expires. `0` means no cap. Useful for a one-off task: share, let the agent make its handful of calls, and know the token is spent."
        },
        "credSshManager.maskAgentOutput": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "default": true,
          "markdownDescription": "Replace this entity's own stored secrets with `<CREDS_MASKED:NAME>` in the output the agent broker returns.\n\n**What this does and does not cover.** It covers output of commands the agent runs *through CredsForDevs* — that is the one channel this extension owns. It cannot cover a file the agent reads with its own tools, anything you paste yourself, or a secret that was already in the model's context. Masking is exact: only values actually stored in this entry, never a guess about what looks like a secret."
        },
        "credSshManager.gitDeployKeys": {
          "type": "object",
          "default": {},
          "additionalProperties": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "markdownDescription": "Which stored SSH key each git remote authenticates with: `{ \"git@github.com:me/vault.git\": \"<entity id>\" }`.\n\nThe key is an ordinary **SSH key** entry in your vault — it is written to the same private `keys/` folder the SSH features already use and purge, so no copy of it is left behind. Leave a remote out and git uses whatever your machine is already configured to do (an agent, a credential helper, an existing deploy key), which is usually what you want."
        }
      }
    },
